Template: mlab-node/api-key
Type: password
Description: M-Lab API key:
 The API key provided by M-Lab for your organization. Nothing is echoed as
 you type it (pasting works). It is written to /etc/mlab-node/api-key.env,
 readable by root only, and is not kept in the debconf database.
 .
 Leave it empty to keep the value already in /etc/mlab-node/api-key.env.

Template: mlab-node/iata
Type: string
Description: IATA code of the nearest large international airport:
 The 3-letter IATA code of the nearest large international airport, in
 upper or lower case (e.g. LHR or lhr). M-Lab names its metros after airports:
 pick the largest international airport serving this machine's
 metropolitan area, not the closest airfield.
 .
 A list of international airports by country, with their IATA codes:
 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_international_airports_by_country

Template: mlab-node/probability
Type: string
Default: 0.1
Description: Probability of being selected by the load balancer (0-1):
 The probability that M-Lab's Locate service selects this node for a
 client in its metro. 1 means as likely as any other M-Lab node in the
 metro; 0.1 (10%) is the recommended starting point. Increase it as you
 gain confidence in how much traffic the node can handle.

Template: mlab-node/interface-name
Type: string
Description: Network interface serving measurement traffic:
 The network interface that measurement traffic leaves through. The rate
 limit below applies to it.
 .
 The proposed value is the interface of this machine's default route.

Template: mlab-node/uplink
Type: string
Description: Uplink speed (e.g. 1g, 10g, 25g):
 The speed of the uplink this machine is connected to, as an integer
 followed by "g" (gigabits per second). The minimum M-Lab requires is 1g;
 10g or faster is recommended.
 .
 The proposed value, when present, is the link speed the interface reports.

Template: mlab-node/interface-maxrate
Type: string
Description: Transmit rate above which new tests are refused (Mbit/s):
 When the interface's transmit rate exceeds this value, ndt-server refuses
 new connections so that running tests are not affected by congestion on
 the uplink. Give it in Mbit/s (e.g. 150, 7000) or in Gbit/s with a "g"
 suffix (e.g. 7g, 2.5g). Recommended: 150 for a 1g uplink, 7000 (7g) for
 10g.
 .
 The proposed value follows those recommendations for the uplink speed
 given above. It is written to /etc/mlab-node/node.env in bit/s.

Template: mlab-node/ipv4
Type: string
Description: Public IPv4 address of this node:
 The IPv4 address clients reach this node at. It is required.
 .
 The proposed value is the address this machine's outbound traffic appears
 to come from.

Template: mlab-node/ipv6
Type: string
Description: Public IPv6 address of this node (optional):
 The IPv6 address clients reach this node at. Leave it empty if the node
 is not reachable over IPv6.
 .
 The proposed value, when present, is the address this machine's outbound
 IPv6 traffic appears to come from.

Template: mlab-node/type
Type: select
Choices: physical, virtual
Description: Machine type:
 Whether this is a bare-metal machine dedicated to M-Lab (physical) or a
 virtual machine (virtual).

Template: mlab-node/unattended-upgrades
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Install security updates and mlab-node upgrades automatically?
 The unattended-upgrades package can install updates on its own, daily.
 If you accept, this machine will automatically install the distribution's
 security updates and new versions of the mlab-node package (when the
 M-Lab APT repository is configured in the APT sources). The stack is
 restarted gracefully after an mlab-node upgrade.
 .
 The settings are written to /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/52mlab-node-unattended-upgrades.

Template: mlab-node/invalid-value
Type: error
Description: Invalid value
 The value entered for ${setting} is not valid: ${reason}
